The migration tool is starting from step 1 because it has no record of the steps already done on your laptop. The app's tables exist; they were just created some other way. So you tell it which step the database has already reached, then let it run only what's missing. For you that should be just step 5, the catalog tables.

Step 1: check which tables exist. Run this in PowerShell. It asks for your postgres password; type it at the prompt rather than pasting it here.

& "C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\17\bin\psql.exe" -U postgres -h 127.0.0.1 -d askdb_app -c "select to_regclass('public.conversations') as s2, to_regclass('public.insight_feedback') as s3, to_regclass('public.saved_analyses') as s4, to_regclass('public.catalog_refreshes') as s5;"
A table name in a column means that step's table exists; a blank means it doesn't.

Step 2: record the last step that's already in place. Run this in apps\api, using the last column that shows a table name:

Last column with a table	Command
s4 (most likely)
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic stamp 0004_saved_analyses
s3
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic stamp 0003_insight_feedback
s2
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic stamp 0002_activity_schema
none (only users exist)
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic stamp 0001_app_schema
stamp only writes a note in the database saying "migrated up to here". It doesn't create, change or delete any tables.

Step 3: run the missing steps.

.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m alembic upgrade head
The output should include Running upgrade 0004_saved_analyses -> 0005_entity_catalog.

Then reload the Entity Catalog tab and click Refresh Catalog. If step 1 shows a table in s5 or looks wrong, paste its output here.

